A||N||12||Xtreme Fiero's||Light weight Flywheels||||5||
Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||10-26-2000||10:25 PM||||<BR>Does anybody make them? is there an advantage to geting one?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||10-26-2000||11:42 PM||||Advantage - less rotating mass = more speed revs faster.<BR>Disadvantage - Less rotating mass = less momentum. Which means that the motor will lose speed under load, making it harder to launch if your motor has no low end torque and will lose speed quicker when going up hills and stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||10-27-2000||07:28 AM||||Yes -- less rotating mass means quicker throttle response but easier to stall.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||10-27-2000||11:02 AM||||Here is an actual and true performance evaluation as written by somebody who has done this mod.<P>Hey everyone,<BR>I'm the guy that had brought up the extra power that the aluminum flywheel gives.<BR>I made the purchase from the Fiero Shop more specifically George Miller the owner.<BR>I can say the driving characteristics are exactly the same, except for when you accelerate aggressively. That is when you will notice the rate of acceleration is most definitely increased you will go through the gears more quickly and it has most certainly got more felt power! I don't feel any<BR>power pulsation's or anything different at all in it's operation that is undesirable. It drives as it did before the mod.<BR>This mod only shows up when you use it....<BR>It is felt in the seat of the pants, the car pushes you back in the drivers seat and takes off like you won't believe, unless you do the mod.<BR>In  short the best modification that I've done so far for my Fiero.<BR>Mike (in Delaware)<BR>1985 GT (HOT 2.8 L "Awesome aluminum Flywheel by the Fiero Shop")<BR>See my engine at Wudman's under the "MODS" (page 1)<BR>Go here:  <A HREF="http://www.thisisfun.com/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thisisfun.com/fiero.htm</A> <P><P>------------------<BR> <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||10-27-2000||09:56 PM||||Fieroshop is right. It is one of the best modifications you can do because you gain horsepower normally lost to rotating a heavy flywheel and lose nothing. Why do you think people spend so much money on lighter wheels and carbon fiber driveshafts? The less rotating mass the motor has to overcome the more horsepower there is left to propel the car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||10-27-2000||11:19 PM||||I think you won't go any faster but you'll get there sooner.<P>Does that make any sense?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||10-28-2000||12:41 AM||||In other words it improves your acceleration....<P>Interesting....<P>How much do they go for George?||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||10-28-2000||12:56 AM||||I've planned on doing this ever since I was into Ford Probes years ago.. I read a lot of stuff from Unorthodox Racing about underdrive pulleys, lighter flywheels etc..<P>In addition to eventually getting a lighter flywheel, I think I'll get an underdrive pulley for the AC, but I'll leave the alternator and cooling system alone.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>unless I get an electric water pump... it shouldn't be a problem as I'm going to have 2 batteries in parallel anyway for the stereo!<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 10-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||fieroshop||10-28-2000||12:56 AM||||The complete setup goes for $925.00 plus shipping. This includes, aluminum flywheel, pressure plate, throwout bearing and kevlar clutch.<BR>This has proven to be the best setup in the market.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||10-28-2000||01:00 AM||||hmm.. I just realized something, all of the lightweight flywheels I've talked to people about have been on manual trans cars...<P>they do work with autos, right?? not dependent on the trans? (dumb question probably.. but I'm tired. lol)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000010||Phil||10-28-2000||09:49 AM||||Autos don't use flywheels they use a thing called a flex plate which is a very light weight disk that holds the ring gear for starting.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||10-28-2000||03:08 PM||||DOH!<P>Is the flex plate lighter than a lighter weight flywheel? if so, does that mean an auto Fiero can rev faster than a manual one with a stock flywheel?<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000012||88formula||10-29-2000||08:28 PM||||No because of the heavy torque converter attached to it. I know what you are thinking but the pressure plate is not as heavy as the torque converter.||||reg||1
